                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2740057b-3e5a-34da-8f45-568140c03c59",
                        "entry_id": "6bd724f2-7632-38f6-b7fa-56150d6c5dd6",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Synonym of excursus."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f2b61385-93b6-4940-b595-ae1954669a8a",
                                "sense_id": "2740057b-3e5a-34da-8f45-568140c03c59",
                                "text": "By the labors of the Bridgewater writers, the argument has been cleared of many extraneous and unnecessary excurses."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02bb79b8-9b9a-413d-818d-cb9900639705",
                                "sense_id": "2740057b-3e5a-34da-8f45-568140c03c59",
                                "text": "Prof. Kuhn shows further that the original version seems to have been better preserved in the Arabic text, at least so far as the end of the tale is concerned. In two Excurses, pp. 40–45 and 45–50, he examines the Hebrew version of [Abraham ben Samuel ha-Levi] Ibn Hisdai (c. 1250) and the Greek; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "40d44489-f812-41f8-982a-128ad4cbc27f",
                                "sense_id": "2740057b-3e5a-34da-8f45-568140c03c59",
                                "text": "Actually, the passage occurs in an excurse in the edictal commentary, the exact meaning of which is unknown; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "36ebc45e-ad04-4641-9311-13d4d2ac6aaf",
                                "sense_id": "2740057b-3e5a-34da-8f45-568140c03c59",
                                "text": "Sometimes, there are overlong excurses in drawing out parallels with other Victorians."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ae4197bf-77e9-36ee-8c55-7ad9126a96fd",
                        "entry_id": "6bd724f2-7632-38f6-b7fa-56150d6c5dd6",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An act of moving or rushing forward or out; an incursion by troops."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d51f39d-e672-4c06-a42d-2b458889cee3",
                                "sense_id": "ae4197bf-77e9-36ee-8c55-7ad9126a96fd",
                                "text": "Ad hæc fratetnitati^([sic]) tuæ præcipiendo modo quo te in Cantuarienſem Eccleſiam recipias, & paucis quidem clericis retêtis admodū neceſſariis, ad minus quam poteris per terram illam diſcurras."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2bb2275f-707d-4975-b940-79f9db154aba",
                                "sense_id": "ae4197bf-77e9-36ee-8c55-7ad9126a96fd",
                                "text": "But foꝛſomuch as now not caring foꝛ thy oth, noꝛ regarding the ſafetie of thy hoſtages, thou haſt ſo fondlie ⁊ lewdlie paſſed thy bounds: I am to require thée, that thou doo retire and withdꝛaw theſe excurſes of ſtrangers; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4405c5b7-e7d1-4994-a9df-592e434f0344",
                                "sense_id": "ae4197bf-77e9-36ee-8c55-7ad9126a96fd",
                                "text": "As a Twigg, after inflexion, doth return beyond the middle, or line of directneſs, and goes and comes frequently, till it hath overcome the firſt impreſſed motion, and recovered its natural ſite becauſe after the firſt Reflexion is made, a ſecond ſucceeds, for the ſame reaſon, as the firſt, a third for the ſame reason as the ſecond, and ſo a fourth, fifth, &c. ſucceſſively: So alſo, is it neceſſary, that many Vibrations, or Excurſes and Recurſes be alternately made, by a Chord diſtended and percuſſed; becauſe the ſame cauſe remains to the ſecond, third, fourth, &c. which was to the firſt."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
